        {
          "_type": "Contribution",
          "_fossil": "contributionMetadata",
          "id": "35",
          "db_id": 7317468,
          "friendly_id": 35,
          "title": "Towards a Standardized Benchmark for Vision-Language Models in CMS Data Quality Monitoring",
          "startDate": {
            "date": "2026-10-21",
            "time": "15:10:00",
            "tz": "Europe/Zurich"
          },
          "endDate": {
            "date": "2026-10-21",
            "time": "15:30:00",
            "tz": "Europe/Zurich"
          },
          "duration": 20,
          "roomFullname": "TBD",
          "room": "TBD",
          "location": "CERN",
          "type": null,
          "description": "The Compact Muon Solenoid (CMS) experiment at the Large Hadron Collider generates a large volume of complex data, making manual inspection of Data Quality Monitoring (DQM) plots by shift takers increasingly difficult. Agentic AI systems built on vision-language models (VLMs) are rapidly being developed to address this, as they can interpret DQM plots against shift instructions and deliver actionable diagnostic guidance. Their growing role in detector operations makes rigorous benchmarking essential, yet no common evaluation standard exists to compare such systems or to quantify their reliability. We present ongoing work toward a benchmark framework for VLM-based DQM assistance in CMS. The framework combines a curated dataset of DQM plots from multiple subsystems, the associated shift instructions as a knowledge base, and human-written reference assessments. Model outputs are scored against these references for plot interpretation, reasoning, and the final diagnostic decision, together with operational metrics such as latency. We evaluated several VLMs within the framework and recorded the best score obtained for each. The benchmark follows community standards for scientific machine learning benchmarks. We provide this benchmark, with these best scores as reference baselines, for agentic systems under development for CMS such as Archi, to be compared against.",
