        {
          "_type": "Contribution",
          "_fossil": "contributionMetadata",
          "id": "38",
          "db_id": 7317484,
          "friendly_id": 38,
          "title": "What OpenSearch offers & how CERN runs it",
          "startDate": {
            "date": "2026-10-20",
            "time": "11:30:00",
            "tz": "Europe/Zurich"
          },
          "endDate": {
            "date": "2026-10-20",
            "time": "12:00:00",
            "tz": "Europe/Zurich"
          },
          "duration": 30,
          "roomFullname": "40/S2-A01 - Salle Anderson",
          "room": "40/S2-A01",
          "location": "CERN",
          "type": null,
          "description": "Behind most knowledge bases and LLM agents there is a retrieval problem: finding the right information quickly, and only what the user is allowed to see. OpenSearch, an open-source (Apache 2.0) search and analytics engine, has grown from a scalable full-text engine into a platform that covers this ground in one place. This talk gives an overview of what OpenSearch offers for knowledge bases and agents. We start from keyword search and its limits, then cover dense and sparse vector search with several engines and compression options, hybrid search, and models hosted in the cluster or reached through external connectors such as the CERN AI Gateway. We show how ingest and search pipelines extract, chunk and embed documents, and then rewrite queries, fuse scores and re-rank results, without application code. On the agent side we present the available agent types, agentic search that turns natural-language questions into queries, built-in and external MCP servers, agentic memory, and Agent Health for observing and evaluating agents.\r\n\r\nWe then present the CERN OpenSearch service: over a hundred clusters and more than a petabyte of data, run as a service for the CERN and WLCG communities, with single sign-on, e-group-based access control and a support model for users. We close with why OpenSearch is a good fit for knowledge bases and agents.",
